ll number of unique  values  now you may know all these levels in  advance maybe not  but the point is that number of levels  is going to be a lot smaller than you  might expect for a character vector  so let's see some examples here i'm  actually going to start by creating  these two vectors x1 and x2 and when you  create vectors like this  they're going to default to just being  character vectors  and all i'm doing is storing the  abbreviations for months i've got  december  april march and january but in this x2  here  the january abbreviation has a typo in  it see we've got jam instead of jan  now the levels for factors are going to  be incredibly important  and in this next chunk of code i'm going  to create this vector  called month levels and it's going to  have  all 12 month abbreviations january  through december  we're going to store that and then in my  next chunk of code  i'm basically going to convert x1 and x2  into  factor variables y1 and y2  now the factor function has this  argument levels  and i'm going to pass to that this  vector month levels  so if we do that let's see what we get  so first thing to point out here is that  these levels  are in the exact same order that i  specified earlier  january through december that's the  order of the months in the year  but you'll also see here that in y2  here that jam typo was dropped  so we just have an n a here now the  order of these levels is incredibly  important and i'm going to show you why  that is in a second  but first let's say we just used the  factor function and we didn't specify  the levels  now if you do that let's just create a  new vector called f1 here  by default the levels are going to be in  alphabetical order  so notice the levels here go from april  december  january march that's alphabetical order  here  so the other way that you could do it  now you may want to go about it this way  but let's say to the levels argument you  pass  the unique function so pass the unique  values of x1  to it and then what's going to happen is  the levels are going to be in the order  that they appear in the data  we specify the order december april  march and january and then lo and behold  that's the order that they show up in  the levels now it's easiest to see why  the order of these levels is important  if we make some simple graphs so to do  this i'm going to generate some fake  data  and let's just pretend this data that  i'm creating are just  counts of some variable that's defined  on a month by month basis  and now what i'm going to do in each of  these code chunks is  i'm going to create a data frame where  one variable is the factor  that is the month and in the other  variable i have these counts  that is the fake data that i just  generated okay  and i'm going to create some simple bar  charts for each of these  so let's start with the case where i  specified that month  levels vector where i ordered  all the months in the order that they  occur in the year  and you'll see that this looks exactly  like how we probably want it to  we have the months in the order that  they occur in the year january march  april december this looks pretty good  but in these other situations look with  this alphabetical case  that's the default when you use the  factor function  it goes april december january march  that's probably not something that you  would want to present to a stakeholder  and similarly if you're going by the  order that these things first appear in  the data  you've got december april march january  again that's probably not what you want  so again just to  bring this home here the order of these  levels is really important and sometimes  you just have to specify that manually  luckily though the forecast package  is going to give us a ton of tools and  useful helper functions  for dealing with the order of factors so  we're going to see some examples here  and what we're going to use here is this  built-in data set called gss  underscore cat so let's take a look at  what this data  actually look like it's got a number of  survey variables you've got things like  year  marital status age race reported income  political party id religion uh religious  denomination  and then hours of tv watched so the  first function i want to point out  is this count function so if you've seen  my dplyr  tutorial you're familiar with using  group by  and then tally so those functions  think of count as just a wrapper for  that set of functions  so what's what this is going to do is if  we just do data  pipe and then count of this variable  marital  it's going to give us the counts of each  of these  unique values for the marital variable  so that's very quick this is just two  lines of code  alternatively though this can make it  even easier you can use the add  count function and that's going to add  these counts directly  to your original data so think of this  as just like a join  but just making it significantly quicker  and easier  so i'm going to run this chunk of code  i'm going to use add count instead of  count  and then notice for these individual  levels of marital  the counts for which these occur  have been added to this to this data  frame so  super helpful now let's look at a simple  bar chart of this variable  so we've got marital status we it goes  no answer  never married separated divorced widowed  married in that order now you might be  thinking  these are somewhat out of order maybe i  want to organize this  bar chart here to where it goes from  most frequent to least frequent or vice  versa  well four cats is going to give us tools  for doing that  so we can use this function fct in freak  stands for infrequently obviously to  reorder the levels of the factor from  most to least frequent  so what i'm going to do here is i'm  going to wrap the marital variable  inside of the fct infrequent  uh function now when we do that  notice we go from most frequently  occurring to least frequently occurring  now another very useful function here is  going to be the fct  rev function obviously again that stands  for  factor reverse and this is going to work  really well with the previous function  because  what fct reverse is going to do is just  the current order  of the levels that you have it's going  to flip that  so before when we were ordering from  most to least frequent  after we've done that if we just reverse  that it's going to go from least to most  frequent  now when you start chaining functions  together like  this it might be useful just to do this  up front rather than wrap  everything inside your ggplot functions  so i'm going to use the deploy our  mutate function  not going to rename the variable or  anything i'm just going to take marital  reorder it from most to least frequent  and then reverse that to get least to  most frequent  do that and then well look at that it  goes from least to most frequent  suddenly this is this is starting to  look pretty clean now so far everything  we've been dealing with has been pretty  one-dimensional  right we've only been working with one  variable at a time  but this concept can be extended to the  case of multiple variables meaning  forecast is going to allow us to reorder  the levels of a factor  based on the values of another variable  so to illustrate this  let's create a new data frame here  called relig  summary now all this is is basically  just a group  summary so i have all the unique  religions from our data frame  and then we have the average age the  average tv hours  and then the counts now let's start by  creating a scatter plot of tv hours by  religion  so by default the way this is ordered is  on the y-axis for a scatter plot  you're going to have the first occurring  factor first  and then it's gonna work its way up so  the order that these things occur in the  data goes no answer  don't know enter non-denominational  so on and so forth now there's no clear  pattern here  but if we pass the fct reorder function  into ggplot what you're gonna see happen  is  the levels of this factor are going to  be rearranged  so they're rearranged from  by tv hours so it goes don't know  has the highest tv hours on average  followed by native american  so on and so forth but then maybe you  also want to modify the specific  location of one or more levels  like let's say here maybe there's no  answer level  maybe we want that to come first for  some reason  now the function that's going to help us  out here is going to be called fct  re-level so here's what i'm going to do  here because i want to make  more than one modification to the  religion variable  i'm going to make those modifications to  the data frame up front  okay so i'm going to start with the  exact same procedure to religion that we  did before  we're going to reorder the religion  variable by the tv hours variable  and then i'm going to use the fct  re-level function next  so if we don't specify any other  arguments no answer is going to come  first  naturally there are other arguments that  you could pass to fct re-level if you  want to manually specify  where exactly in the order of levels  that that goes  but let's see what happens okay  so as i explained before actually the  first level of that factor is going to  be at the bottom of the scatter plot  and as you see here we successfully  moved the no answer level  to that position so that just about  covers it for modifying the order  of the levels of factors but 4cats is  also going to help us if we want to  modify  the actual levels themselves and we're  going to see an example here  if we go back to the original data we  were working with and we look at the  party id variable  so it'll be clear pretty quickly this  variable is  kind of a mess so if you see here we've  got  not str republican they kind of  abbreviated the word strong  there's no capitalization of the word  republican  and notice it goes end comma near with  no spaces  this doesn't look very good we want to  clean this up  so if we use the fct recode function we  can basically create a mapping  where we have first we're going to have  the new values  and then we're going to have the old  values on the right so  where we had strong republican before we  want to have republican  comma strong not str republican is going  to turn into republican  comma weak so on and so forth we do that  and only those ones that we modified got  changed  and all of a sudden this physically  looks quite a lot cleaner  now what we could do next is actually  collapse these different levels  so go from many levels to fewer levels  the fct collapse function is going to  help us to do that  so instead of where we have no answer  don't know  other party we want to just have one  level  other instead of republican strong or  republican weak  we just want to have republican let's do  that  see what that looks like and bam instead  of having a bunch of levels we only have  four levels so we've really cleaned this  thing up  then the last thing that i'm going to  show you is the fct  lump family of functions and there is  actually a function just  fct lump but the creators of the  forecast package  do recommend at this point use these  helper functions which are more specific  instead now what these functions are  going to do is  they're going to make it very quick and  easy and convenient for us  to just lump these infrequently  occurring levels  into some category just called other  now there are a number of ways that we  can do this we have this function  fct lump min which is going to lump  levels that appear fewer than  you specify the number of times you also  have this function  fct lump n that's going to lump all  levels except for the n  most frequently occurring levels  together so let's see an example of that  one  i'm going to take that marital variable  that we were working  with before and i want to lump together  all but the three most frequently  occurring values  into an other category let's do that  and then bam the most frequently  occurring things were  married divorced uh and never married  and then everything else just appears  under other  now so again that's a lot cleaner and  this can really help out if you have a  lot of levels for some reason  so this should cover the most helpful  functionality out of the forecast  package  and as always with my r tutorials this  is not a comprehensive overview of  every single function out there for that  reason  get used to calling the help  documentation and maybe look on crayon  at the 4k package to see all the  different functions that are available  just so you can see everything that you  can possibly do  but this should help you have the tools  at your disposal to deal with the real  world problems that you face  get used to dealing with factors because  they show up all the time  and they really are a beautiful class of  data in the r  ecosystem so as always thanks for  watching this video  if you found it helpful please smash the  like button  and also consider sharing this video  then i'll see you all in the not so  distant future  until then richard on data

